#{[17592186222499 "An alternative, but equivalent, definition for the **order** can be made in terms of the local truncation error.\n\nA numerical method has **order** $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has **order** $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nBut why is the power $p+1$ for LTE but only $p$ for GTE?\n\nEach local truncation error measures the error made in *one step* of the method. If a method has order $p,$ then the error made in a single step shrinks like\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nHowever, on a fixed interval, using step size $\\Delta x$ requires about\n\n$$\nN \\approx \\dfrac1{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nsteps. The global truncation error results from *accumulating* these local errors over all steps.\n\nHeuristically, adding up $N$ local errors of size $(\\Delta x)^{p+1}$ gives\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\sim N \\cdot (\\Delta x)^{p+1} \\sim \\dfrac1{\\Delta x} \\cdot (\\Delta x)^{p+1} = (\\Delta x)^p.\n$$\n\nThus, the local truncation error is one power of $\\Delta x$ higher than the global truncation error.\n\nNext, we will apply this definition to determine the order of a given numerical method." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222500 "Suppose we have a system of ODEs such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\mathbf{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwith a nonzero equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ satisfying\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nInstead of analyzing the system near a *nonzero* equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ we can introduce *shifted coordinates* so that the equilibrium becomes the origin.\nWe define the **deviation vector**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nso that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0} \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nThis transformed system has several advantages: shifting the equilibrium to the origin makes the system easier to analyze without altering its behavior.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following system of ODEs:\n\n![](images/47/4788b3521295a9a2ccdd0840940ff1c27263bb580ce72d787a06e1e1af50b9af.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, -1]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/04/04406c96333cbaabb72b49ad48aac2b144e2f21c01be688c1ad5f0cb6eda5391.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n![](images/a7/a7d302836d0991995492abd0ccb35bb2701836fe4a4f50decfdd0866e7fecfae.svg)\n\nNotice that for linear systems, shifting the equilibrium to the origin just removes the constant term." #edb/ref 13194139535987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222501 "Recall that when we shift coordinates by defining\n\n$$\nu = x - x^\\ast,\\qquad v = y - y^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwe translate the coordinate system so that the equilibrium $(x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ becomes $(0,0).$\n\nThis translation preserves several important properties of the system. Shifting coordinates does not change the dynamics of the system, it only changes where the equilibrium appears in the coordinate plane. In particular:\n\n1\\. *Eigenvalues do not change:*\n\n  After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors are unchanged.\n\n2\\. *Stability type is preserved:*\n\n  The equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.). Shifting does not alter the local behavior near the equilibrium.\n\n3\\. *Trajectory shapes are unchanged up to translation:*\n\n  Trajectories in the $(u,v)$-plane have the same shapes as those in the $(x,y)$-plane. Only their location changes.\n\n4\\. *Equilibria correspond one-to-one:*\n\n  The target equilibrium $(x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ maps to $(u,v) = (0,0).$ The shift does not create or remove equilibria.\n\n5\\. *Nullclines translate but retain the same shape:*\n\n  The geometric structure of the vector field is preserved under translation.\n\nLet's proceed to some examples." #edb/ref 13194139534668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222502 "Consider the homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nA **fundamental matrix** for this system is an $n\\times n$ matrix $\\Phi(t)$ whose columns are $n$ linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)$ are linearly independent solutions, then one fundamental matrix is\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)\\big].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ can be written in any order.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222503 "Again, let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The fundamental matrices have the following important properties.\n\n1\\. *Satisfies the underlying system*. Indeed, since each column of $\\Phi(t)$ is a solution of the system, we have\n\n  $$\n  \\Phi'(t) = A\\,\\Phi(t).\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Invertibility*. The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t,$ so\n\n  $$\n  \\det\\Phi(t)\\neq 0\n  $$\n\n  for all $t.$ In particular, $\\Phi(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$\n\n3\\. *Representation of solutions*. Every solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system can be written in the form\n\n  $$\n  \\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,\\mathbf{c},\n  $$\n\n  where $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\n4\\. *Non-uniqueness*. If $C$ is any invertible constant matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  \\Psi(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,C\n  $$\n\n  is also a fundamental matrix for the same system.\n\nWe'll now get some practice." #edb/ref 13194139534883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222504 "For a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with constant $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **standard fundamental matrix** is the unique fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ that satisfies the initial condition\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor constant matrices $A,$ the standard fundamental matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix satisfies\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t)=A\\,\\Phi_0(t)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Any other fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for the same system can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an invertible constant matrix.\n\nIn the next slide, we will compute the standard fundamental matrix for a specific example." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222505 "Again, let $A$ be a constant $n\\times n$ matrix. The matrix exponential satisfies\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\bigl(e^{At}\\bigr)=A\\,e^{At}.\n$$\n\nIndeed, recall that\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(At)^k}{k!}\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{A^k t^k}{k!}.\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we differentiate the series above term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k}\\cdot k{t}^{k-1}}{k!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k}{t}^{k-1}}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =A{\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k-1}{t}^{k-1}}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =A{\\sum }_{j=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{j}{t}^{j}}{j!} \\\\  & =A\\,{e}^{At}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222506 "Matrix exponentials have other properties. We list a few of them:\n\n- *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n$$\ne^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n$$\n\n- *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n(e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n$$\n\n- *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n$$\ne^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n$$\n\n- *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n$$\ne^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute." #edb/ref 13194139533443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222507 "A pair of solutions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ to a $2\\times 2$ homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis called a **fundamental set of solutions** on an interval if the solutions are linearly independent on that interval.\n\nA practical test for determining whether a pair of functions forms a fundamental set of solutions is to use the Wronskian: \n\n> If $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the system and\n> \n> \n> $$\n> W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2) \\neq 0\n> $$\n> \n> \n> for at least one $t_0$ in the interval, then they form a fundamental set of solutions on that interval.\n\nThis definition generalizes to any $n\\times n$ homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).$ A set of solutions\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\ \\mathbf{x}_2(t),\\ \\ldots,\\ \\mathbf{x}_n(t)\n$$\n\nis a *fundamental set of solutions* on an interval if the solutions are linearly independent on that interval.\n\nThe same Wronskian idea applies: compute the determinant of the $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\ldots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t).$ If it is nonzero at some $t_0$ in the interval, then the solutions form a fundamental set on that interval.\n\nNext, we will look at a concrete example applying this test." #edb/ref 13194139533444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222508 "Recall that the first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nThis means the new Laplace transform is the original graph of $F$ evaluated at $s-a$.\n\nSo, the graph shift is:\n\n- If $a>0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the right** by $a$ units.\n\n- If $a<0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the left** by $|a|$ units.\n\nFor example, suppose $F(s)$ has the following graph:\n\n![](images/45/45ce62c981e4c7853ef158046eb21fbc9c135f5fa0a8887d813a07594077c8ce.png)\n\nThen the Laplace transform of $e^{-3t} f(t)$ is $F(s+3).$ Here, $a=-3$, so this corresponds to shifting the graph of $F(s)$ three units to the left:\n\n![](images/b7/b75658824079fe90c9c4f43d4d37d355c95b845ebed3f11bdf751b52f8d34f55.png)\n\nA useful self-check is that any horizontal features move the same way:\n\n- vertical asymptotes shift left/right by the same amount,\n\n- intercepts and key points shift left/right by the same amount." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222509 "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." #edb/ref 13194139534857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222510 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |  |  |\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222511 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and of exponential\norder $a.$ This means that there exist constants $K>0$ and $T>0$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)| \\le K e^{a t}\n\\qquad \\text{for all } t > T.\n$$\n\nWe define the Laplace transform of $f$ by\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}(s)\n     = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $s > a,$ then for all sufficiently large $t,$\n\n$$\n|e^{-st} f(t)| \\le K e^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nSince $s-a>0,$ the function $e^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty).$\nIn addition, because $f$ is piecewise continuous, it is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTogether, these observations show that the integral defining $F(s)$ converges,\nso $F(s)$ is well-defined for all $s>a.$\n\nNow fix $s>a$ and compute the derivative of $F(s).$ Starting from the definition,\n\n$$\nF'(s)\n= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}s}\n  \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe exponential-order assumption ensures that this integral converges, which allows us to differentiate under the integral sign.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nF'(s)\n= \\int_0^\\infty\n    \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial s}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\n  \\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $f(t)$ does not depend on $s,$ applying the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial s}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\n= -t e^{-st} f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integral, we obtain\n\n$$\nF'(s)\n= -\\int_0^\\infty t e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, by definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty t e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\mathcal{L}\\{t f(t)\\}(s).\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF'(s) = -\\mathcal{L}\\{t f(t)\\}(s),\n$$\n\nwhich proves the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nRepeating this argument shows that the Laplace transform is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222512 "We'll now show that the definition of order with respect to LTE is the same as with respect to GTE.\n\nIndeed, suppose a numerical method with increment formula $\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x$ satisfies the following:\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nOn a fixed interval of length $L,$ the number of steps is $N \\approx \\dfrac{L}{\\Delta x}.$ So,\n$N = \\mathcal O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\Delta x}\\right)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$\n\nNow, recall the relationship between GTE and LTE, given by\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N = e_{N-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nSince $y(x_{N-1}) - y_{N-1} = e_{N-1},$ it is natural to say that changing the input $y$ by $e_{N-1}$ changes $\\Phi$ by about a constant multiple of $e_{N-1}.$ So, heuristically,\n\n$$\n\\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\approx Ce_{N-1}\n$$\n\nfor some $C$ that *doesn't* depend on $\\Delta x$ (on a fixed interval). Hence, the GTE satisfies approximately\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)e_{N-1} + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nAfter $N$ steps, we get\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)^N e_0 + \\sum_{n=1}^N(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}\\tau_n.\n$$\n\nNext, note that $(1+C\\Delta x)^N \\approx e^{CN\\Delta x} \\approx e^{CL},$ which is $\\mathcal O(1)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$ So, the weights $(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}$ don't change the *power* of $\\Delta x.$\n\nSo, we can treat the GTE as roughly the sum of the per-step local errors. Therefore,\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx \\sum_{n=1}^N \\tau_n\n$$\n\nWith $\\tau_n = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)$ for each $n=1,\\ldots,N,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(Δx) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =N\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,(\\frac{1}{Δx})\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,((Δx)^{p}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p$ is maximal in the LTE definition, this shows the GTE has order $p$ as well." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222513 "A particularly important special case occurs when $f(t)=1$. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\n\\qquad s>0,\n$$\n\nwe can use the smoothness property to compute **Laplace transforms of powers of $t$** by *repeated differentiation with respect to $s$*.\n\nFirst, applying the smoothness property once,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t\\}=-\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)\n=\\frac{1}{s^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nApplying it again,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^2\\}\n=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d} s^2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)\n=\\frac{2}{s^3},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nApplying it a third time (noting that $3 \\times 2 = 6 = 3!$),\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^3\\}\n=-\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^3}{\\mathrm{d} s^3}\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right)\n=\\frac{6}{s^4} = \\frac{3!}{s^4},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nFrom these examples, a clear pattern emerges. For every integer $n\\ge 0$,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^n\\}=\\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nThis formula allows us to compute the Laplace transform of *any polynomial* by combining linearity with this result." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222514 "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)cos\\,(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,d\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 13194139534500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222515 "When studying differential equations, we often face expressions like \n\n$$\nt\\,e^{kt},\\qquad t\\,\\sin(\\omega t), \\qquad t^2\\,\\cosh(kt)\n$$\n\nor higher powers of $t$ multiplying familiar functions. The Laplace transform gives a systematic way to handle these by converting “multiply by $t$” into “differentiate with respect to $s$.”\n\nIn this topic, we focus on a key regularity fact: even if $f(t)$ has corners or jumps, its Laplace transform $F(s)$ is *perfectly smooth* as a function of $s$ (within its region of convergence). This is called the **smoothness property** (or the **differentiation of transforms** property).\n\nFirst, let's assume that\n\n- $f(t)$ is *piecewise continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$, and\n\n- $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a$ (so $|f(t)| = O(e^{at})$ as $t\\to\\infty$).\n\nThen the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for all $s>a$, and $F$ is *infinitely differentiable* with respect to $s$ on this interval.\n\nMoreover, for every integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a$,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^n f(t)\\}(s)=(-1)^n F^{(n)}(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F^{(n)}$ denotes the $n$th derivative of $F$ with respect to $s$.\n\nIn particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t f(t)\\}=-F'(s),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L\\{t^2 f(t)\\}=F''(s).\n$$\n\nThis matters because it turns multiplication by powers of $t$ into differentiation with respect to $s$, which is often much faster than re-integrating from scratch or applying integration by parts multiple times.\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this property to work through a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222516 "In previous lessons, we derived several basic Laplace transforms directly from the definition.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Domain |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{s}$ | $s > 0$ |\n| $e^{at}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{s-a}$ | $s > a$ |\n| $e^{\\mathrm i \\omega t}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{s-\\mathrm i \\omega}$ | $s > 0$ |\n| $\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ | $s > 0$ |\n| $\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ | $s > 0$ |\n| $\\sinh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2 - \\omega^2}$ | $s > |\\omega|$ |\n| $\\cosh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s}{s^2-\\omega^2}$ | $s > |\\omega|$ |\n| $u(t-a), \\ a \\ge 0$ | $\\dfrac{e^{-as}}{s}$ | $s > 0$ |\n\nOne goal of this lesson is to expand this table by using the **smoothness property**. In the next slide, we will apply this property to derive a new transform without computing integrals from scratch." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222517 "Using the smoothness property, we can now add transforms involving factors of $t$ and higher powers of $t$ by differentiating previously known formulas.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Domain |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $t e^{kt}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{(s-k)^2}$ | $s>k$ |\n| $t^2 e^{kt}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{(s-k)^3}$ | $s>k$ |\n| $t\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2}$ | $s>0$ |\n| $t^2\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2\\omega(3s^2-\\omega^2)}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>0$ |\n| $t\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s^2-\\omega^2}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2}$ | $s>0$ |\n| $t^2\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2s(s^2-3\\omega^2)}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>0$ |\n| $t\\sinh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^2}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n| $t^2\\sinh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2\\omega(3s^2+\\omega^2)}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n| $t\\cosh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s^2+\\omega^2}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^2}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n| $t^2\\cosh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2s(s^2+3\\omega^2)}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n\nFinally, note that we have a shortcut pattern for exponentials. If\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{kt}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\n\\qquad s>k,\n$$\n\nthen repeated differentiation shows that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^n e^{kt}\\}=\\frac{n!}{(s-k)^{n+1}},\n\\qquad s>k.\n$$\n\nThis pattern is a direct consequence of the smoothness property and provides a fast way to compute many common transforms." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222518 "Let's use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of $\\mathcal L\\{t e^{-9t}\\}.$\n\nIf a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = e^{-9t}.$ Therefore, for $s > -9,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+9}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{s+9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s+9)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s+9)^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{-9t}} & =-{F}^{'}(s) \\\\  & =-[-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}},\\,s>-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222519 "When a Fourier series represents a function $f$, it is often much easier to differentiate the series term-by-term than to recompute a brand-new Fourier series from scratch. \n\nHowever, term-by-term differentiation is only guaranteed to work when the underlying periodic function is sufficiently \"well-behaved\".\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term differentiation of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is\n\n- piecewise smooth, and\n\n- continuous.\n\nThen, the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} -\\dfrac{n\\pi a_n}{L}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+\\dfrac{n\\pi b_n}{L}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $f(x)$ is the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ defined on $x \\in [-L,L),$ then the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x)$ provided that\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-L,L),$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-L,L),$ and\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -L^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to L^-} F(x).$\n\n**Note:** The constant term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}$ disappears after differentiating, since $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this theorem to a specific example to see how it works in practice." #edb/ref 13194139534557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222520 "Using the first shifting theorem, we can obtain new Laplace transforms from old ones without performing any additional integration.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThe table below shows some general results we can obtain using the first shifting theorem.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Compare With |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $e^{at} t^n$ | $\\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}$ | $\\mathcal L \\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n\nSo, each new entry in our table is obtained by taking a known transform and replacing $s$ with $s-a$, and then shifting the domain accordingly." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222521 "Just like differentiation, integration can also be applied directly to a Fourier series. This is often useful when we already know the Fourier series of $f(x)$ and want a series for an antiderivative of $f(x)$ without recomputing Fourier coefficients from scratch.\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term integration of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is piecewise continuous. Then, an antiderivative $F(x)$, where $F'(x) = f(x)$, can be found by integrating the Fourier series of $f(x)$ term-by-term:\n\n$$\nF(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}x + C + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\bigg( \\dfrac{a_n L}{n\\pi}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)-\\dfrac{b_n L}{n\\pi}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg) \\bigg)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n**Watch out!** Note the following caveats:\n\n1\\. The expression for the antiderivative $F(x)$ is not a Fourier series in the usual sense because it contains the linear term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}x.$\n\n2\\. Even if $f(x)$ is periodic, its antiderivative $F(x)$ is not necessarily periodic. In particular, if $a_0 \\neq 0,$ then the term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}x$ grows without bound as $x$ increases.\n\nIf we want a periodic antiderivative, we typically need the average value of $f$ over one period to be $0$ (equivalently, $a_0=0$), so that the linear term disappears.\n\n**Note:** Integration is generally \"safer\" than differentiation for Fourier series. Differentiation can slow convergence or even destroy it, whereas integration divides the $n$th coefficient by $n,$ which typically improves convergence.\n\nNext, let's see a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139534557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222522 "Suppose a linear ODE has a periodic forcing term whose Fourier series is known, for example\n\n$$\ny' + ky = f(x),\n\\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad\nf(x) \\sim \\frac{c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(c_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + d_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\nA standard strategy is to look for a *particular* solution with the *same Fourier frequencies*:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(a_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big)\n$$\n\nThen, the method includes the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Differentiate term-by-term.\n\nUsing $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\cos(\\omega_n x)=-\\omega_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)$ and $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sin(\\omega_n x)=\\omega_n\\cos(\\omega_n x)$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(-a_n\\omega_n\\sin(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\omega_n\\cos(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Substitute into the ODE and collect like terms\n\nThen, we plug $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the equation, and rewrite the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{free} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{term}}\\Big) \\;+\\; \n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{cosine} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{coefficient}}\\Big) \\cos(\\omega_n x) \\;+\\; \n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{sine} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{coefficient}}\\Big) \\sin(\\omega_n x).\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Equate coefficients.\n\nBecause the Fourier series representation is unique (up to convergence issues we ignore here), we match:\n\n- the free terms, and\n\n- for each $n\\ge 1$, the cosine coefficients and sine coefficients.\n\nThis produces:\n\n- one equation for $a_0$ (from the constant term), and\n\n- a $2\\times 2$ linear system for $(a_n,b_n)$ for each $n\\ge 1$.\n\n- **Step 4:** Write the Fourier series of $y_p.$\n\nAfter solving for $a_0$, $a_n$, and $b_n$, substitute them back into\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(a_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222523 "For second-order linear ODEs, the overall algorithm is the same as for first-order equations: \n\n- we assume a Fourier series for $y_p$,\n\n- substitute into the ODE, and\n\n- match coefficients.\n\nBefore we start, we can use the forcing series properties to simplify our work. For a second-order equation of the form \n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution can be simplified.\n\nThe key insight comes from the fact that the differential operator \n\n$$\nL[y] = y'' + \\omega^2 y\n$$\n\nmaps cosines to cosines and sines to sines. This means we can match the form of our assumed solution $y_p$ to the form of the forcing function $f(x),$ which saves a great deal of work.\n\nThis leads to some powerful shortcuts:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is a *pure cosine series* (an even function), we can assume $y_p$ is also a pure cosine series (all $b_n=0$).\n\n- If $f(x)$ is a *pure sine series* (an odd function), we can assume $y_p$ is also a pure sine series (all $a_n=0$, including $a_0=0$).\n\n- If the constant term in the series for $f(x)$ is zero, the constant term in $y_p$ will also be zero (so $a_0=0$).\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222524 "We begin with the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a) g(t)\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nIn many applications, the function multiplied by the unit step already appears in the shifted form\n\n$$\nu(t-a)\\,f(t-a).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the theorem so that this structure can be handled directly.\n\nSuppose $f(t)$ is a function with Laplace transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).\n$$\n\nTo match the form $u(t-a)f(t-a)$, define\n\n$$\ng(t)=f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThen the left-hand side becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a) g(t)\\}\n=\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, determine what happens to the right-hand side.\nIf $g(t)=f(t-a)$, then\n\n$$\ng(t+a)=f\\big((t+a)-a\\big)=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}\n= e^{-as}F(s).\n$$\n\nThis is the **alternative form of the second shifting theorem**.\n\nNext, we will apply this formula to a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222525 "Assume that\n\n- $f(t)$ is *piecewise continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$, and\n\n- $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a$.\n\nThen the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for all $s>a$, and we have the **Integration Property**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\!\\left\\{\\int_0^t f(\\tau)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau\\right\\}\n= \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n\\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nThis result shows that *integrating a function with respect to time* corresponds to *dividing its Laplace transform by $s$*.  \n\nThis result allows us to handle accumulated quantities without re-integrating from scratch.\n\nNext, we will apply this property to a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222526 "The **unit step function** $u(t-a)$ acts like a switch:\n\n- If $t < a$, then $u(t-a)=0$.\n\n- If $t \\ge a$, then $u(t-a)=1$.\n\nSo, when we multiply a function $g(t)$ by $u(t-a)$, the function becomes\n![](images/99/99f4e68acca148ef5e6c69f20bba890868d3dbe8e75f0616e77ba5fc1bb27d87.svg)\n\nThis operation \"switches on\" the function $g(t)$ at time $t=a$.\n\nFor example, consider the function $y(t)=u(t-2) \\cdot \\dfrac14 t^2.$ Here, $a=2$ and $g(t) = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2,$ so\n![](images/b4/b4f287198bb074d3a90853f3418e5f606d7ec022770eef76ebbd5b54aeb60aec.svg)\nThe plot of this function is shown below. Note that the curve follows the parabola for $t \\ge 2$ but is zero for $t < 2$.\n\n![](images/04/04398ba96ee11b0f99405b6890ec2c16070460e45bbe7aec1630ec27698d0b93.png)\n\nSuppose we know the Laplace transform of $g(t).$ We'll now learn how to find the Laplace transform of $u(t-a)\\,g(t).$" #edb/ref 13194139535981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222527 "Term-by-term differentiation is a huge time-saver: it lets us obtain a Fourier series for $f'(x)$ without recomputing Fourier coefficients from scratch. \n\nBut differentiation also tends to make Fourier series \"less convergent\", it \"spends\" one power of $n$. So, we need to be careful about what the differentiated series actually does.\n\nIn a $2L$-periodic Fourier series, differentiating \n\n$$\n\\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right) \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nintroduces a factor of $\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}$. So, very roughly,\n\n$$\n\\big(\\text{new coefficient size}\\big) \\approx n \\cdot \\big(\\text{old coefficient size}\\big).\n$$\n\nThat means one level of coefficient decay is \"used up\" when we differentiate.\n\nFor example, consider the so-called *triangle wave* and *sawtooth wave*, the $2\\pi$-periodic extensions from $x \\in  [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ of the functions $y=|x|$ and $y=x,$ respectively. \n\n![](images/90/90341fcc0828ee1fbefa229a1dc8b0a55ebf5912cfaf934585c73dae5ab720d0.png)\n\nTheir Fourier series are the following:\n\n$$\n|x| \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos\\!\\big((2n-1)x\\big)}{(2n-1)^2}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nx \\sim 2\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nThe convergence of their corresponding derivatives is summarized in the table below.\n\n| **Function Type** | **Continuity** | **Fourier Series Coefficient Decay** | **Derivative Series Convergence** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Triangle wave | Continuous | $O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right)$ | Converges \n(to the average of slopes) |\n| Sawtooth wave | Discontinuous | $O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)$ | Diverges |\n\nNotice that:\n\n- For the continuous and piecewise smooth *triangle wave*:\n\n- The function has enough \"smoothness\" (continuity) that its Fourier coefficients decay quickly at a rate of $\\sim \\dfrac{1}{n^2}.$\n\n- Differentiation \"costs\" one level of decay: \n\n$$\nn \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n^2} = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nSince the terms $\\dfrac{1}{n}$ still approach $0,$ the series can converge. At the \"corners,\" it settles on the average of the left- and right-hand slopes.\n\n- For the discontinuous but piecewise smooth *sawtooth wave*:\n\n- The function's jump discontinuities \"use up\" the smoothness budget. The coefficients decay only at a rate of $\\sim \\dfrac{1}{n}.$\n\n- Differentiation cancels out the decay entirely: \n\n$$\nn \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n} = 1\n$$\n\nSince the terms of the series do not approach $0,$ the series fails the basic divergence test and does not converge." #edb/ref 13194139533444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222528 "A forcing harmonic can cause *resonance* when its frequency matches the natural frequency of the homogeneous equation.\n\nIn cases like that, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the natural frequency.\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2 y = f(x),\n$$\n\nthe homogeneous solution oscillates with natural frequency $\\omega$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Find the forcing frequencies.\n\nIf $f$ is $2L$-periodic, then its Fourier series uses harmonics\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhose frequencies are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\frac{n\\pi}{L}\\qquad(n\\ge 1).\n$$\n\nA harmonic is *resonant* exactly when $\\omega_n=\\omega$.\n\n- **Step 3:** Form the particular solution ansatz for each harmonic.\n\n- For a *non-resonant* harmonic ($\\omega_n\\ne \\omega$), we use the standard ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_{p_n}=A\\cos(\\omega_n x)+B\\sin(\\omega_n x)\n$$\n\n- For a *resonant* harmonic ($\\omega_n=\\omega$), this form overlaps with the homogeneous solution. We must use the *modified ansatz* by multiplying by $x{:}$\n\n$$\ny_{p_n}=x\\left(A\\cos(\\omega x)+B\\sin(\\omega x)\\right)\n$$\n\nThis extra factor of $x$ produces a new trial function that can match the forcing term.\n\nWhen $f$ is given as a Fourier series, we check each harmonic:\n\n- *non-resonant* harmonics contribute ordinary sine/cosine terms to $y_p,$ while\n\n- *resonant* harmonics contribute terms multiplied by $x.$\n\nIn particular, the resonant harmonic is the one with index $n$ satisfying\n\n$$\n\\frac{n\\pi}{L}=\\omega.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." #edb/ref 13194139533445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222529 "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186222530 "Consider the differential equation $y''-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause $f$ has no free term and is a pure cosine series, we assume\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n n^2\\pi^2\\cos(n\\pi x)\n-\n4\\left(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)\\cos(n\\pi x)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n-a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)=\\dfrac{2}{n^2}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a Fourier series for a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\: -\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)} \\cdot \\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222531 "Once the first iteration of an implicit Euler method implementation is complete, we can continue applying the same general update formula iteratively.\n\nIn our previous example, we used one iteration of the implicit Euler method on the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = x-y+4, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nusing the general formula\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = \\dfrac12(y + x_\\text{new} + 4)\n$$\n\nto approximate the value of $y(1)\\approx3.$ Let's now approximate the value of $y(2)$ with one more step.\n\nSince the step size $\\Delta x = 1$ remains the same, the update formula derived in the previous step still applies.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have $x=1$ and $y=3.$ Hence, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + 1 = 2,$ and the new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{2}(3+2+4)=\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx \\dfrac92.$ We can visualize this second step as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29ae501c0693f352f2bcae1b0f310380a31a6387063104991f52aaebdde7a919.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222532 "Once the first iteration of a trapezoidal method implementation is complete, we can continue applying the same general update formula iteratively.\n\nIn our previous example, we used one iteration of the trapezoidal method on the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2,\n$$\n\nusing the general formula\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = x + x_\\text{new} + 3y + 2\n$$\n\nto approximate the value $y(1)\\approx9.$ Let's now approximate the value of $y(2)$ with one more step.\n\nSince the step size $\\Delta x = 1$ remains the same, the update formula derived in the previous step still applies.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have $x=1$ and $y=9.$ Hence, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 1+1=2,$ and the new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+2+3\\cdot 9+2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 32.$ We can visualize this second step as follows:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9752c16b15ebcfcfa214c775e99fb68139a1dfa9364a5f552fbf676d7953f7.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226234 "In this lesson, we’ll practice applying the zero-product rule to solve quadratic equations." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226235 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may face quadratics that don’t easily factor. In such cases, developing fluency with the quadratic formula can save crucial time.\n\nBelow, we'll practice writing quadratic equations in standard form and applying the quadratic formula." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226236 "Vieta’s formulas are often helpful in algebra problems that ask for the sum or product of roots of a quadratic equation, but don't require the roots themselves. By utilizing Vieta's formulas, we can solve these problems quickly and avoid lengthy computations.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice applying these formulas, helping us master one of the quickest shortcuts in quadratic problem-solving." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226237 "Trigonometry problems sometimes require us to find a missing side or trigonometric ratio when only two sides of a right triangle are given. These questions test the ability to connect the Pythagorean theorem with sine, cosine, and tangent definitions.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice using the Pythagorean theorem to complete missing triangle information and then find trigonometric ratios." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226238 "Problems involving special triangles (equilateral, isosceles right, and $30^\\circ$–$60^\\circ$–$90^\\circ$ triangles) are common in both geometry and coordinate setups.\n\nHere, we’ll practice recognizing these triangle types and apply their relationships to find unknown sides, heights, and areas." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226239 "Sometimes, we may encounter multi-step geometry problems that require using similar triangles and trigonometric ratios together to find missing sides or distances.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll get hands-on practice setting up proportions, applying trigonometric ratios, and solving for unknowns." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226240 "Questions that require adding or subtracting rational expressions with variables and common factors in their denominators test fluency in recognizing common factors. In this topic, we’ll practice combining rational expressions involving common monomial and binomial denominators, including cases where we must first factor some of the denominators." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226241 "Simplifying rational expressions using polynomial factorization tests our ability to spot, factor, and cancel common polynomial terms.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice simplifying rational expressions by factoring trinomials, differences of squares, and common binomials." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226242 "In this topic, we'll practice identifying exponential functions from tabular data. In some cases, the base of the function may be a decimal or defined in terms of a parameter." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226243 "Questions involving tables of values often test whether we can recover a quadratic pattern from data.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice interpreting tabular data to determine equations of quadratic functions and their key features." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226244 "This topic provides practice in solving rational equations using three methods: cancelling common factors, applying the flip method, and multiplying through by the lowest common denominator." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226245 "This topic focuses on practicing solving radical equations, including cases where the radical must be isolated first and where variables appear on both sides. We'll also practice finding unknown parameters that result in exactly one real solution." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226246 "This topic helps us build proficiency in solving equations containing multiple radicals. The key idea is to write the equation in such a way that squaring both sides eliminates the radicals. In some cases, we will find a solution in terms of an unknown parameter." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226247 "In this topic, we'll practice evaluating simple cubic functions and use our knowledge of $n$th root equations to find the input of a cubic that produces a given output." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226248 "This topic provides practice in analyzing absolute value functions. In particular, we'll evaluate these functions, determine inputs for given function outputs, and find unknown constants or parameters in related equations." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226249 "This topic provides practice in evaluating and analyzing rational functions. We'll determine outputs of rational expressions, identify the equations of shifted reciprocal functions, and recognize how transformations affect their graphs." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226250 "This topic provides practice in analyzing exponential functions by evaluating them for given inputs, identifying y-intercepts, interpreting tables of values, and recognizing their graphical representations." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226251 "Questions about graphs of quadratic functions commonly ask to recognize key features like intercepts and the vertex from a graph or equation.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll practice spotting these patterns." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226252 "A typical question type on the SAT math section involves interpreting key features of graphs in real-world contexts.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice determining the meaning of points and interpreting the $x$- and $y$-intercepts in terms of the situation they represent." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226253 "The math section of the SAT includes questions that require us to interpret graphs modeling real-world relationships.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying points corresponding to specific descriptions, determining initial values, recognizing intervals where a function increases or decreases, and describing changes in a function’s value based on its graph." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226254 "This topic provides practice in solving systems composed of a quadratic equation and a linear equation." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226255 "In this topic, we'll focus on solving challenging systems composed of a quadratic equation and a linear equation." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226256 "In this topic, we'll strengthen our ability to solve multivariable rational equations. We will use cross-multiplication, isolate variables appearing in numerators and denominators, and handle equations containing several rational terms." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226257 "This topic focuses on solving multivariable equations that require two or three steps to isolate a chosen variable." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226258 "In this topic, we will practice constructing exponential models from descriptions involving doubling times, percentage increases, and growth rates greater than 100 percent, and evaluating these models for given inputs." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226259 "After solving exponential models for the growth rate or the initial value, we'll now focus on solving for time. In particular, we'll practice calculating how long it takes for quantities to double or triple, identifying exponential equations given in context, and finding unknown times from tables or given models." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226260 "In this topic, we will practice writing and evaluating equations representing percentage decreases, half-lives, and other decay rates in real-world situations. Some questions will also involve determining unknown decay rates or modeling vertically shifted decay functions." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226261 "A common theme on the math section of the SAT is working with exponential models that contain unknown parameters, such as the growth rate or the initial value. In this topic, we'll practice solving for these unknowns given a description of a real-world situation." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226262 "In this topic, we'll practice transforming exponential functions, rewriting the transformed functions using the laws of exponents." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226263 "Another common theme in the math section of the SAT is constructing exponential functions from a given description. Here, we'll practice building exponential models from descriptions that include initial values, growth factors, or percentage decreases." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226264 "Questions that ask you to determine inputs for a given output in quadratic functions are a common feature of the math section of the SAT. In this lesson, we'll gain valuable practice with these problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226265 "Quadratic models describing real-world phenomena often have specific features that can be interpreted.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice translating quadratic models into plain-English statements." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226266 "Geometry problems that involve dimensions, area, or perimeter often lead to quadratic equations.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll practice translating geometric descriptions into quadratic equations and solving them." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226267 "Questions about extreme values of quadratic functions in standard form often test whether we know how to find a vertex.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice the efficient application of the vertex formula." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226268 "Problems involving extreme values of quadratic functions often test whether we can quickly identify the vertex of a parabola from its factored form.\n\nHere, we’ll practice efficiently locating maximums and minimums of quadratic functions presented in factored form." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226269 "Problems that relate two or more numbers through multiplication or addition often lead to quadratic equations. These problems test our ability to translate a word problem into a quadratic equation and solve it.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll practice forming and solving quadratic equations that model number relationships. The resulting quadratic equations typically have quite large coefficients, which often make the quadratic formula an efficient solution method." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226270 "When presented with real-world data on vertical motion, we can often fit it to a quadratic model using vertex form. In this topic, we'll practice doing just that." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226271 "Questions about multiplying binomials test the fluency with the distributive property and the ability to organize terms correctly to avoid sign or arithmetic errors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice expanding products of binomials of different forms, simplifying the results, and matching coefficients or terms to solve for unknown values." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226272 "Questions involving distributive laws often appear on the math section of the SAT exam. These questions check whether we can correctly expand, combine, and simplify expressions.\n\nHere, we’ll practice applying the distributive property to polynomial expressions, simplifying them, and identifying or comparing coefficients to find missing values." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226273 "The difference of squares formula often appears in questions that require quick recognition of algebraic patterns.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice identifying and factoring differences of squares, including cases with coefficients, fractions, and multiple variables." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226274 "Factoring by a greatest common factor (GCF) is often used as a step in solving problems that contain polynomial expressions.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice spotting numeric and variable GCFs in polynomials and using them to factor the expressions." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226275 "Questions involving factoring trinomials are common on the math section of the SAT exam. Recognizing the corresponding patterns can help us solve equations and simplify expressions.\n\nHere, we’ll practice factoring different types of trinomials, including perfect squares and more general quadratic expressions." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226276 "Factoring higher-order polynomials tests our ability to recognize patterns beyond simple quadratics.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying and factoring polynomial patterns, including higher powers and nested differences of squares." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226277 "Problems involving borders or frames often test the ability to express areas with unknown widths using algebraic equations.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice modeling these geometric relationships and solving for missing dimensions." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226278 "The math problems often involve modeling area or perimeter using algebraic functions. These questions combine geometric understanding with algebraic manipulation.\n\nMastering this connection between shapes and equations takes practice, but it’s a powerful way to boost both reasoning speed and accuracy when dealing with geometry problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226279 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we're often asked to create a function for surface area or volume directly from a description.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice constructing and interpreting functions that represent surface area and volume, turning real-world descriptions into precise mathematical formulas." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226280 "Problems involving inscribed squares and circles often test our understanding of how geometric relationships between these shapes connect.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice translating between circle and square parameters (radius, side, area, and circumference) to solve multi-step geometry problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226281 "On the math section of the SAT exam, questions about circles inscribed in equilateral triangles often depend on 30-60-90 right-triangle reasoning. In this lesson, we’ll practice setting up these relationships and solving problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226282 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we’ll often see problems that hide special triangles inside rectangles, squares, or circles. These questions test our ability to spot these special triangles and use their ratios to find unknown lengths or areas.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice recognizing these patterns and setting up the correct equations." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226283 "Inscribed‐circle problems often ask us to switch quickly between a circle’s radius or diameter and a square’s side.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice setting up these relationships and solving multi-step geometric problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226284 "Problems involving equilateral triangles inscribed in a circle often boil down to recalling 30-60-90 triangle facts.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice setting up these relationships and solving related problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226285 "The math section of the SAT exam sometimes asks to evaluate simple trigonometric functions at large angles. These evaluations are made by reducing the argument of the trigonometric function using periodicity.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice reducing large angles to their coterminal or reference angles, identifying the relevant quadrants, and determining the correct sign and value of the trigonometric ratio." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226286 "Questions that require us to solve quadratic equations with unknown parameters show up regularly on the math section of the SAT exam.\n\nIn this topic, we'll build speed by practicing systematic moves for solving these kinds of problems." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226287 "Solving quadratic equations by factoring tests the ability to recognize when a quadratic can be factored quickly into linear factors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice rewriting, factoring, and solving quadratic equations efficiently, developing the fluency needed to identify patterns and avoid time-consuming methods like the quadratic formula." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226288 "This topic focuses on understanding how the structure of a quadratic expression reveals useful information about its behavior.\n\nBy recognizing connections across different forms, we’ll be able to extract relationships between coefficients, factors, and intercepts." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226289 "Quadratic equations have exactly one real solution when their discriminant equals zero.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying and using this condition." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226290 "Quadratic equations have real solutions when their discriminant is nonnegative, and *distinct* real solutions when their discriminant is positive.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying and using these conditions." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226291 "Quadratic equations have no real solutions when their discriminant is negative.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying and using this condition." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226292 "This topic focuses on solving nonlinear equations that involve multiple variables. We'll practice isolating variables in radical and quadratic equations, rearranging formulas, and applying the laws of exponents when working with radicals." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226293 "In this topic, we'll practice solving systems of nonlinear equations involving square roots, exponentials, and absolute values." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226294 "An important concept that features in the math section of the SAT exam is identifying when a line and a parabola intersect at exactly one point.\n\nIn this topic, we will determine unknown parameters in quadratic systems with one solution involving horizontal or sloped lines, as well as cases where both linear and quadratic equations within a system contain parameters." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226295 "Common questions on the math section of the SAT exam involve working with exponential functions written in equivalent forms.\n\nYou'll need to be able to analyze how different representations display key quantities, such as constant terms, coefficients, or bases, that correspond to the function's maximum, minimum, or given output values." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226296 "In this topic, we'll practice converting exponential models to different time units. We will adjust equations to reflect new time measures, compute percentage increases or decreases over specified intervals, and determine time periods associated with particular percentage changes." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226297 "A common theme on the SAT math section is understanding how exponential functions behave, and this can include cases with restricted domains. In particular, you will need to identify when they have maximum or minimum values, determine their ranges, and describe whether they are increasing or decreasing over those domains." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226298 "A common theme on the math section of the SAT is working with exponential functions that contain one or multiple unknown parameters.\nIn this topic, we'll practice solving for these unknowns using information such as points on the curve or the y-intercept." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186226299 "In this topic, we'll practice performing horizontal and vertical translations on polynomials." #edb/ref 13194139533383 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
